Here are top 5 hashtag#Terraform practices, battle-tested in real-world environments:

1. Use Remote State with Locking
Never store state files locally, especially in a team or CI/CD setup. Use S3 + DynamoDB (AWS), Terraform Cloud, or Azure Storage with locking enabled to avoid conflicts and ensure consistency.

2. Modularize for Reusability
Turn repeatable patterns (like VPCs, IAM, ECS, etc.) into modules. It keeps your code hashtag#DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself), enforces standards, and accelerates provisioning across environments.

3. Separate Environments Clearly
Workspaces are good, but folder-based separation (e.g., live/test, live/dev, live/prod) offers better clarity and CI/CD integration. Reduces the risk of “oops” moments.

 4. Enforce Code Quality and Policies
Use tools like tflint, tfsec, checkov, and terraform fmt. Integrate them into your pipelines. Infrastructure is code, treat it like it.

5. Tag Everything + Document Inputs/Outputs
Tags help with cost allocation, audits, and readability. Document module inputs/outputs with clear descriptions. terraform-docs is your friend here.

These practices have helped me build more secure, maintainable, and scalable infrastructure in fast-moving environments.
